[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The “invisible bridge” holding the two halves together is an OSPF virtual-link between r4 and r8 over area 1. After one of the boxes restarted, that virtual-link adjacency briefly dropped, which temporarily removed all area 2 reachability from the backbone, making the “far” prefixes disappear. Evidence: r4 shows an OSPF neighbor on VLINK0 to 18.1.4.1 and r8 shows VLINK0 to 14.1.4.1; both are Full, but their Up Time counters were significantly lower than all other adjacencies (≈48–60s vs >1m), indicating a recent bounce. Additionally, r4’s routing table contains OSPF-learned routes to r8’s loopbacks (18.1.x.1) across the virtual-link, confirming it is the path that restores those networks. End-to-end reachability from pc0 to pc12 is currently healthy (pings succeed both ways), so the outage was transient during adjacency re-formation, not a persistent fault. Root cause: reliance on an OSPF virtual-link (a logical “bridge”) means if either endpoint process restarts or the transit area momentarily fails, the backbone loses the far area’s LSAs. Corrective fix: eliminate the dependency on the virtual-link by providing a real area 0 path (e.g., add a physical or point-to-point area 0 link between the ABR side near r8 and the backbone), or redesign so r8’s area 2 connects to area 0 via a direct ABR. As a mitigation, ensure FRR auto-start is enabled and OSPF router-ids are explicitly set (to avoid RID changes), and consider BFD on the transit links to speed convergence. 
=== END ===